    {
      "id": "e8472847213",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 3490,
      "width": 1326,
      "height": 170,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 271884546,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 399670336,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790005987766,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1  Name the thing, not its shape \u2014 OutboundEmail / InboundEmail, never Email + a direction flag.\n2  Command = intent, imperative, ONE consumer who owns the schema.  Event = fact, past tense, ONE publisher, 0..n subscribers.\n3  Never name an event after something that didn't happen.  Put the reason in the data: bounceReason = RECIPIENT_NOT_FOUND.\n4  Events that must stay in order share one topic and one key.  Commands and events never share a topic.\n5  Consumers dispatch on messageType and skip what they don't know \u2014 schema compatibility protects the format, only the consumer protects the meaning.\n6  BACKWARD_TRANSITIVE everywhere.  Add fields only with defaults.  A breaking change means a new topic, never a lowered setting.\n7  Blocking retries on ordered topics \u2014 a non-blocking retry topic lets later events overtake the failed one.\n8  A bounced REGULATORY email is a compliance fact: evidence goes to the archive, and the sending context falls back to another channel.",
      "originalText": "1  Name the thing, not its shape \u2014 OutboundEmail / InboundEmail, never Email + a direction flag.\n2  Command = intent, imperative, ONE consumer who owns the schema.  Event = fact, past tense, ONE publisher, 0..n subscribers.\n3  Never name an event after something that didn't happen.  Put the reason in the data: bounceReason = RECIPIENT_NOT_FOUND.\n4  Events that must stay in order share one topic and one key.  Commands and events never share a topic.\n5  Consumers dispatch on messageType and skip what they don't know \u2014 schema compatibility protects the format, only the consumer protects the meaning.\n6  BACKWARD_TRANSITIVE everywhere.  Add fields only with defaults.  A breaking change means a new topic, never a lowered setting.\n7  Blocking retries on ordered topics \u2014 a non-blocking retry topic lets later events overtake the failed one.\n8  A bounced REGULATORY email is a compliance fact: evidence goes to the archive, and the sending context falls back to another channel.",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true
    }
  ],
